From aa78fd41b50b60621c9a95b4c7509a47cd73ecf9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@local>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 22:14:59 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 22/23] feat(debug): record what happens when the screen goes
 black

A black screen on this device is currently unattributable: `body` is #09090b, so
an unmounted React tree and a live app whose GL layers stopped drawing look
identical, and there is no adb to ask (services/debug/frameSamples.ts makes the
same argument for frame timings). Every path that can blank the screen now
leaves a line of text.

`services/debug/incidents.ts` keeps a 64-entry ring and persists its tail, so the
evidence outlives the document that produced it - the interesting case is the one
that needed a reload. Three failure modes go into it:

- uncaught exceptions and unhandled rejections, installed before createRoot so
  the first commit is covered;
- WebGL context loss, captured at the document because the event does not bubble
  and the canvases that lose contexts belong to third-party shader plumbing.
  Deliberately NOT `preventDefault`ed: that promises the browser a restore these
  layers cannot perform, which would trade "nothing drawn" for "restored context
  drawing nothing";
- a blank root, detected by counting #root's children twice a second. React
  unmounts the whole tree on an uncaught render or effect error, and with no
  error boundary in this app that state IS the black screen.

Only the blank root raises the overlay, and that overlay is plain DOM appended to
the document rather than a React component: nothing React can render exists in
that state. It lists the recent incidents and offers a reload, so the failure
becomes recoverable instead of terminal. Ordinary thrown handlers are recorded
but never covered up - the app still works, and a red box would be worse than the
exception.

The frame HUD gains `gl` (contexts lost / restored), `err` (count and the last
message) and `blank`, plus a `reload` button: when the screen is black but the
tree is alive, the HUD is the one surface that can still reach the user, and it
already has a gesture everyone knows.
